  "identifier": "NG2927",
  "full_title": "The Coronation of the Virgin; The Trinity; The Virgin and Child with Donors; The Crucifixion; The Twelve Apostles",
  "short_title": "Scenes of the Virgin; The Trinity; The Crucifixion",
  "object_name": "Picture",
  "style": "Gothic",
  "material": "egg tempera on wood",
  "dimensions": "82 × 60.7 cm",
  "production_date": "1374",
  "brief_description": "It is unusual to see these four images grouped together like this. This arrangement and the evidence of hinges at the panel's right edge suggest that it was once part of a larger work made up two or more connected panels.\n\nClockwise from top left, we see the coronation of the Virgin (when Christ crowned his mother the Queen of Heaven); the Trinity (God the Father, Christ, his son, on the Cross, and the Holy Ghost in the form of a dove); and Christ's crucifixion.\n\nThe two figures who kneel before the Virgin and Child in last scene have been identified as Juana Manuel, Queen of Castile in Spain, and her father, Juan Manuel. These were Barnaba da Modena's patrons. By the time this picture was made he had already painted another for their chapel in the cathedral of Murcia, a Spanish town.",
  "accession_date": "1913-01-01",
  "current_location": "Room 65, The National Gallery (London)",
  "credit_line": "Presented by Rosalind, Countess of Carlisle, 1913",
